      "id": 99533,
      "name": "Chaosmancer",
      "username": "Chaosmancer",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/chaosmancer/{size}/1977_2.png",
      "created_at": "2014-04-17T03:59:23.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"boxwood\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/b/eb8c5e/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> boxwood:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Well it is pretty clear that the Egyptian relics have have had some physical effects on those who works them, take Shu, Geb, and several others as an example before raising that tomb that whole team were normal humans, now we have a ghost lady and a snake headed guy.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>With this, it is possible that Ra is still Blake Washington. The nose and general build are still similiar enough for me. The biggest difference is in skin tone, and that could simply be a combination of the Staff making Washington more \"Egyptian\" and the fact that tactics seems to take place… what, at least 5 years down the road? You'd get very, very tanned spending multiple years out in the desert sun with magic.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>I think I prefer that \"Ra\" is becoming more dominant and changing Washington's body to look more like his body from thousands of years ago, after all he's been relying on this power an awful lot and the Darkwatch Nightmist theory with Anubis' rod also effects physical changes with relying on this egyptian power.</p>",
      "post_number": 11,
